@* 
PSEUDOCODE / PLAN (detailed):
1. The view currently declares a single `Product` as the model but uses `foreach` over `Model`.
2. Fix by changing the model type to a collection: `IEnumerable<DemoWeb1.Models.Product>`.
3. Keep the existing markup that iterates: `@foreach (var pro in Model)`.
4. For each `pro`:
   - Render `ProductID`.
   - Render an image using `@Url.Content(pro.Image_Pro)` to resolve virtual paths.
   - Provide a "Buy" button (keeps as input submit for minimal change).
   - Render `NamePro` and `Price`.
5. Ensure Razor directives and HTML structure remain valid.
*@

@model IEnumerable<DemoWeb1.Models.Product>

@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Index";
}

<h2>DANH SACH SAN PHAM</h2>
<div class="container">
    <div class="row">
        @foreach (var pro in Model)
        {
            <div class="col-sm-12 col-sm-6 col-md-4 col-lg-3">
                <div class="card-pro">
                    <p>@Html.DisplayFor(modelItem => pro.IDProduct)</p>
                    <img src="@Url.Content(pro.NamePro)" height="150" width="150" alt="@pro.NamePro" />
                    <p><input type="submit" value="Buy" /></p>
                    <p>@Html.DisplayFor(modelItem => pro.NamePro)</p>
                    <p>@Html.DisplayFor(modelItem => pro.Price)</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
</div>
